+# Agent Note: Unavailable historical attachment request projection
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-07-unavailable-attachment-request-projection.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+A restored session can retain a valid `ImageAttachmentRef` while its content-addressed object is absent from local storage. Both image-capable adapters attempted to prepare every retained reference, so one absent object prevented a later text prompt from reaching the model.
+
+## Decision
+
+`dsh-llm` provides the stable unavailable-image placeholder and recursive transient projection. The direct DeepSeek and pi-ai adapters catch only `AttachmentError` with `ATTACHMENT_NOT_FOUND` while preparing route-specific image versions, replace every matching direct or nested tool-result image with that placeholder for the current provider request, and leave the durable message unchanged. Other attachment failures still stop the request.
+
+The placeholder records the durable image identity and asks the model to request a new attachment if it needs the image. It is derived from the logged reference, so the request remains reconstructable from the session log even when the referenced object is unavailable.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Change the attachment store to return synthetic image data.** This would hide a missing content-addressed object and make a provider receive bytes that do not match the durable reference.
+- **Rewrite or remove the image block in the session log.** An absent object cannot be recovered from its digest, and modifying append-only history would discard the original reference.
+- **Treat every attachment read failure as unavailable.** Integrity, decoding, and storage failures still require an explicit error because they can indicate corrupted or unsafe input.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A session whose historical image object is missing can continue with text context and an explicit model-visible recovery instruction.
+- Restoring the original attachment object remains the only way to recover the image itself; the request projection does not repair storage.
+- One unavailable attachment changes the provider-request text at that occurrence and can prevent provider cache reuse from there.
